## 7. Pull Request Workflow

### 7.1 Branch naming

```
feat/<short-slug>       new capability
fix/<short-slug>        bug fix
docs/<short-slug>       documentation only
chore/<short-slug>      tooling, deps, CI
refactor/<short-slug>   behaviour-preserving restructuring
```

### 7.2 Conventional commits

```
feat(parser): support nested include directives
fix(cli): exit non-zero when config is malformed
docs(index): add pinned-install instructions
chore(deps): recompile lock files
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`.

### 7.3 Step by step

```bash
# Stay current
git fetch upstream
git switch -c feat/my-change upstream/main

# Work, committing in logical chunks
git add -p
git commit -m "feat(scope): describe the change"

# Rebase rather than merge to keep history linear
git fetch upstream
git rebase upstream/main

# Push and open the PR
git push -u origin feat/my-change
gh pr create --fill --base main
```

### 7.4 What reviewers look for

| Area | Expectation |
|---|---|
| Scope | One logical change per PR; unrelated cleanup goes in its own PR |
| Tests | New behaviour is covered; bug fixes include a regression test |
| Types | Public functions annotated; `mypy` clean |
| Deps | Any new dependency is justified, pinned, and reflected in the lock file |
| Docs | User-visible changes update `docs/` and `CHANGELOG.md` |
| Security | No secrets, no `eval` on untrusted input, no disabled TLS verification |
| Accessibility | UI changes keep keyboard operability and accessible names intact |

### 7.5 PR description template

```markdown
## Summary
<what changed and why, in two sentences>

## Linked issue
Closes #<NUMBER>

## Type of change
- [ ] Bug fix
- [ ] New feature
- [ ] Breaking change
- [ ] Documentation
- [ ] Dependency / tooling

## How it was tested
<commands run, environments covered>

## AI assistance
- [ ] No AI assistance used
- [ ] AI assistance used — tool: <NAME>, scope: <WHAT IT GENERATED>

## Checklist
- [ ] Follows the style guide and passes `pre-commit run --all-files`
- [ ] Tests added or updated, and passing locally
- [ ] Lock files regenerated if dependencies changed
- [ ] Docs and CHANGELOG updated
- [ ] No secrets or personal data included
```

### 7.6 Review etiquette

- Authors: keep PRs under ~400 changed lines where possible; respond to every comment, even if only to say "done" or "disagree because…".
- Reviewers: distinguish blocking issues from nits — prefix nits with `nit:`. Approve when the change is an improvement, not when it is perfect.
- Anyone may open a draft PR early to gather direction before investing effort.

